y in the unit-circle bound forces z^j=1 whenever uj>0. Thus positive weights are supported within {2,4}, {3}, or {5}. These yield P(1) values 8,10,12,14,16, plus 12 and 20. Their sum is 92.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h101"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"],"id":"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts","label":"Turn ordered coefficients into nonnegative differences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"unit-circle-triangle-equality","label":"Equality in the modulus bound","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unit-circle-triangle-equality":["cnm_h101"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h101"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 7","reason":"Taking moduli gives |z|^5=|z|, so z=0 or |z|=1. In the latter case, conjugate(z)=1/z, giving z^6=1. Its six distinct roots all satisfy the original equation, for seven solutions total.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"unit-modulus-reduction","label":"Modulus and conjugate product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unit-modulus-reduction":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"modulus-and-algebraic-roots","label":"Compare moduli, then factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"modulus-and-algebraic-roots":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104","math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-quadratic-solutions"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104","math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-quadratic-solutions"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"polar-angle-count","label":"Compare lengths and angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polar-angle-count":["cnm_h098","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 2004","reason":"Set z=a+bi. The solution z=0 works. For z≠0, taking moduli gives |z|^2002=|z|, hence |z|=1 and conjugate(z)=1/z. The equation becomes z^2003=1, which has 2003 distinct roots. Including zero gives 2004 pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098"],"id":"polar-argument-count","label":"Compare magnitudes and arguments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polar-argument-count":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h097","cnm_h098"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 225","reason":"Equality i^x=(1+i)^y occurs only for y=0 and x divisible by 4, contributing 5*19=95 triples with z different. Equality i^x=z with the other value different contributes another 95. Equality (1+i)^y=z occurs at (y,z)=(0,1) or (8,16), contributing 15+20=35. Total: 225.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"polar-powers","label":"Classify powers by modulus and argument","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polar-powers":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h098"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h098"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (3/2)(√2 − 1)","reason":"The roots are z = −6 + √3 exp(ikπ/4), so the circumradius is √3. The five unordered cyclic-gap types summing to 8 give areas (3/2)(√2 − 1), 3/2, 3√2/2, 3, and (3/2)(1 + √2). The first is smallest and is attained by three consecutive vertices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"octagon-coordinate-areas","label":"Coordinates and shoelace","methodDirectNodeIds":{"octagon-coordinate-areas":["cnm_h098","math:shoelace-formula"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","math:shoelace-formula"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (0, 5)","reason":"Relative to (3,1), the original point has displacement (-4,-3). A 270-degree counterclockwise rotation sends (u,v) to (v,-u), giving (-3,4). Adding the center produces (0,5).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complex-quarter-turn","label":"Rotate using complex numbers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complex-quarter-turn":["cnm_245","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_245","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7","alignment:78c98be15b7f15670862fca0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 7/16","reason":"Squaring a_n+i*b_n gives (3+4*i)^n, whose imaginary part is 2*a_n*b_n. Put z=(3+4*i)/7, with modulus 5/7. For every finite N, direct multiplication gives (1-z)*(1+z+...+z^N)=1-z^(N+1). Thus the requested real partial sum is half the imaginary part of (1-z^(N+1))/(1-z). Its remainder has absolute value at most (1/2)*(5/7)^(N+1)/|1-z|, tending to zero by real geometric decay. Finally 1/(1-z)=7/(4-4*i)=7/8+(7/8)*i, yielding 7/16, B. Reuse this complete finite-complex telescope and real remainder route. The real-ratio infinite-series node is used only for its real majorant; its definition is not widened to complex ratios.","recordedAnswer":"B","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h101"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"finite-complex-geometric-sum","label":"Square and sum finite powers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"finite-complex-geometric-sum":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h098","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h098","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"finite-complex-telescope-real-remainder","label":"Telescope finite complex powers and bound the remainder by a real geometric sequence","methodDirectNodeIds":{"finite-complex-telescope-real-remainder":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h101","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h101","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. -2","reason":"Write z=u+iv and t=|z|². Expansion gives 12t=t²+4(u+1)²+36, hence (t-6)²+4(u+1)²=0. Thus t=6 and u=-1. Since 6/z=conjugate(z), the requested value is 2u=-2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"conjugates-and-real-squares","label":"Use conjugates to expose real squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugates-and-real-squares":["cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_h103","math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_h103","math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: -a","reason":"For every root r, |r| = 1 implies 1/r = conjugate(r). Therefore the reciprocal sum is the conjugate of the root sum, which is conjugate(-a) = -a because a is real.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1987_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_032"],"id":"unit-circle-reciprocals","label":"Turn reciprocals into conjugates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unit-circle-reciprocals":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 2^2005","reason":"All terms have modulus 1, so conjugate(z_n)=1/z_n and z_(n+1)=iz_n². Iteration gives z_N=i^(2^N-1)z_0^(2^N). At N=2005 this is -iz_0^(2^2005), so z_0^(2^2005)=i. This equation has exactly 2^2005 distinct roots, all of modulus 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"],"id":"solution:backward-square-roots:27794b62f4d8","label":"Count predecessors backward","methodDirectNodeIds":{"backward-square-roots":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h231","cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"],"id":"iterate-squaring","label":"Turn the recurrence into repeated squaring","methodDirectNodeIds":{"iterate-squaring":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h200","cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2024","reason":"Add a hypothetical 2016th flight. Each complete 12-flight block has displacement (-6,-12-6√3), so 168 blocks give (-1008,-2016-1008√3). Subtracting flight 2016, whose vector is (1008√3,-1008), leaves (-1008(1+√3),-1008(1+√3)). Its length is 1008√2+1008√6, giving 1008+2+1008+6=2024.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"root-of-unity-displacement","label":"Sum displacements using a twelfth root of unity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-of-unity-displacement":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h102","cnm_h098"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h102","cnm_h098"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 0","reason":"Put z=cos(x)+i sin(x), so |z|=1 and F(z)=z^3-z^2+z+1. If F(z)=0, conjugating and multiplying by z^3 gives z^3+z^2-z+1=0. Subtraction yields 2z(z-1)=0. Since z≠0, this forces z=1, but F(1)=2. Thus there are no such x.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_151"],"id":"unit-circle-conjugation","label":"Use a unit-circle polynomial and its conjugate","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unit-circle-conjugation":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h098","cnm_h103","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_151","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2^(5/4)","reason":"The equation is (z+i)^4=1+i. The four shifted roots have modulus 2^(1/8) and consecutive arguments differing by pi/2. Their square has area 2(2^(1/8))^2=2^(5/4).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_072","cnm_p148","cnm_h039"],"id":"translated-fourth-power","label":"Translate the roots and use quarter-turns","methodDirectNodeIds":{"translated-fourth-power":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_152","cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_072","cnm_p148","cnm_h039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 7/9","reason":"Native mathematical derivation agrees with the recorded choice; no grading change proposed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"],"id":"solution:complex-multiplication-and-corner-areas:621232c0bce0","label":"Subtract the corner triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complex-multiplication-and-corner-areas":["math:geometric-probability","cnm_h102","math:linear-half-plane-systems","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["math:geometric-probability","cnm_h102","math:linear-half-plane-systems","cnm_p150","cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:768f91337a9287bf06fa79a7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 8√2","reason":"P(z)=(z^4-1)(z^4+7+4√3). Its roots are (±1,0), (0,±1), and (±a,±a), where a=(1+√3)/2. The minimum pairwise distance is √2, with a²+(a-1)²=2. Ordering the roots by angle produces eight edges of that length, attaining 8√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_173"],"id":"solution:cartesian-distance-bound:d495bc8865f8","label":"Locate the roots and bound every edge","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cartesian-distance-bound":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_152","cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_173"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h101"],"label":"N-CN.B.6","layer":"US","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"code":"N-CN.B.6","description":"(+) Calculate the distance between numbers in the complex plane as the modulus of the difference, and the midpoint of a segment as the average of the numbers at its endpoints.","id":"625","label":"N-CN.B.6","metadata":{"clusterId":"132","clusterName":"Represent Complex Numbers And Their Operations On The Complex Plane.","clusterOrdinal":"B","domainCode":"N-CN","domainId":"117","domainName":"The Complex Number System","ordinal":"6","sourceCode":"HS.N-CN.B.6"},"parentSourceId":null,"placement":{"grade":"HS","meaning":"High school band, not an exact year"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"625","status":"partial","total":50}
